<p class="no-indent" style="text-align:center; color:#888; font-size:15px; margin-bottom:30px;">Based on LM I:34 (concluded)<br/>Number of Kru'im ֲ· 39 Melachos ֲ· Yom Kippur ֲ· Simchas Torah</p>

<h3>ֲ§28</h3>

<p>Therefore we do not read Torah with fewer than <span class="key-term">three</span> [readers], and we do not read fewer than <span class="key-term">three p'sukim</span> for each reader ג€” corresponding to the three N'kudos. Therefore the entire Torah is <span class="transliteration">m'shuleshes</span> <span class="bracket-def">[tripled/tripartite]</span>, as the Sages said: "<span class="transliteration">Brich Rachmana d'yahiv lan Oryan t'lisa'ah b'yom t'lisa'ah al y'day t'lisa'ah</span>" ג€” "Blessed is the Merciful One who gave us a threefold Torah on a third day through a third [one]" <span class="source">(Shabbos ch. 8)</span> ג€” all corresponding to the three N'kudos that Torah is comprised of.</p>

<h3>ֲ§29</h3>

<p>Therefore on every day that has <span class="key-term">additional kedushah</span>, we call up more readers: on Rosh Chodesh (which has a Musaf) ג€” four; on Yom Tov (which has bitul melachah) ג€” five; etc. The more readers are added, the more the N'kudah can illuminate ג€” for these readers illuminate the N'kudah in all who hear. Therefore one must <span class="bold-concept">incline one's ear and heart well</span> to hear Kri'as HaTorah, and not let one's mind wander, chas v'shalom ג€” and certainly not to speak then! ג€” for his avon is too great to bear, Rachmana litzlan. Through hearing Kri'as HaTorah, one draws upon oneself the illumination of the N'kudah from Torah ג€” on which depends all the tikkun of avonos and desires (from sh'viras kailim), rectified through the light of the N'kudah = tikkun of all worlds.</p>

<p>The more readers, the more the N'kudah illuminates ג€” each person of Yisrael has a unique N'kudah ג†’ through increasing readers, the N'kudah illuminates more from its root in Torah. On Rosh Chodesh (Musaf korban) ג€” four, because the korbanos in the Mikdash (through the Kohain) subdued animal desires (fallen loves) through the N'kudah. On days with a Musaf (Rosh Chodesh, Chol HaMo'ed) ג€” the N'kudah illuminates more ג†’ additional readers.</p>

<p>On <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span> (which has bitul melachah) ג€” yet more: five. Bitul melachah means the kedushah of <span class="transliteration">Shabbos</span> illuminates more in them ג€” when all melachos cease (they come from the <span class="transliteration">zuhamas haNachash</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the contamination of the Serpent]</span>, whose power derives from the k'lipos of sh'viras kailim ג†’ "<span class="transliteration">B'itzavon tochalena, b'zai'as apecha</span>" ג€” "In sorrow shall you eat, by the sweat of your brow" ג†’ all the toil of the <span class="key-term">39 melachos</span>). Due to the sin of Adam HaRishon (causing sh'viras kailim greatly), man must toil in the 39 melachos = tikkun sh'viras kailim (repairing and completing all things = tikkun kailim). Therefore the essential tikkun was through the <span class="transliteration">M'leches HaMishkan</span> ג€” which contained the power of all 39 melachos. The Mishkan = the Bais HaMikdash = where the Aron and Luchos stand = the essential N'kudah ג†’ tikkun sh'viras kailim.</p>

<p>Therefore a person must be careful when engaged in melachah or business (all included in the 39 melachos) ג€” to perform them with great kedushah according to Torah, to draw the N'kudah's illumination into the work and business = tikkun kailim. Then all his work is in the aspect of <span class="transliteration">M'leches HaMishkan</span> <span class="source">(see LM I:11)</span>.</p>

<p>But on <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, all 39 melachos cease ג€” even M'leches HaMishkan does not override Shabbos. Shabbos is the very essence of the N'kudah ג†’ the essential tikkun of all kailim. Therefore it is rest and tranquility ג€” the N'kudah is <span class="transliteration">k'vi'a v'kayma</span> ג€” and the tikkun of all kailim throughout the six weekdays derives its chiyus from Shabbos (= from the N'kudah). Yamim Tovim with bitul melachah are more closely attached to Shabbos ג†’ Yom Tov is also called Shabbos ג†’ more readers to illuminate the N'kudah more.</p>

<h3>ֲ§30</h3>

<p>Therefore on <span class="key-term">Yom HaKippurim</span> we call up even more: six. The kedushah of Yom Kippur is closer to the kedushah of Shabbos than any other Yom Tov ג€” close in the ultimate proximity: "<span class="transliteration">Ain bain Yom HaKippurim l'Shabbos</span>" ג€” "There is no [difference] between Yom Kippur and Shabbos [except...]" <span class="source">(Megillah 7)</span>. Only a small distinction remains between them. On Yom Kippur the N'kudah illuminates enormously ג€” it is the N'kudah of the entire year: "<span class="transliteration">V'lo echad bahem</span>" ג€” "and lo echad bahem" <span class="source">(Tehillim 139)</span>. Therefore the Kohain Gadol enters lifnai v'lifnim to the Even Sh'siyah (where the Aron and Luchos stand) to atone for all avonos.</p>

<p>Six readers = the <span class="bold-concept">vav of the M'lapum</span>, completed then in its ultimate perfection. Then it receives the very essence of the N'kudah from Shabbos (= the completeness of Da'as and Chochmah = yud = the N'kudah itself). This is a general principle: all aspects, middos, and tikkunim exist in generality and detail ג€” every aspect is comprised of all of them (as Rabbainu z"l explains regarding M'lapum existing in klaliyus and p'ratiyus).</p>

<p>Therefore even though the N'kudah illuminates in every day and every person ג€” the ordinary person in relation to the great one/Tzaddik is in the aspect of vav (heart), while the Tzaddik is the yud (N'kudah). So too each day relative to the greater day is vav relative to it. But all days ג€” even all Yamim Tovim ג€” are not in the ultimate completeness of even the vav of M'lapum until they are included in Yom Kippur: the one day per year that includes the entire year, when all avonos are forgiven (for it is the N'kudah relative to them ג€” its kedushah being so close to Shabbos = the N'kudah itself).</p>

<p>Yet all the kedushah of Yom Kippur relative to Shabbos is in the aspect of vav ג€” completed then in ultimate perfection ג†’ receiving the light of the N'kudah of Shabbos Kodesh (<span class="transliteration">k'vi'a v'kayma</span>). Yom Kippur, though exceedingly holy (called <span class="transliteration">Shabbas Shabboson</span>), does not have kedushah that is <span class="transliteration">k'vi'a v'kayma</span> like Shabbos ג€” Yom Kippur's kedushah depends on Yisrael (the Bais Din who sanctify the month ג†’ "<span class="transliteration">M'kadesh Yisrael v'Yom HaKippurim</span>" ג€” Yisrael sanctify it). But Shabbos is <span class="transliteration">k'vi'a v'kayma</span> ג€” the very N'kudah drawn from Hashem Himself: "<span class="transliteration">Shabbos ג€” Sh'ma d'Kudsha B'rich Hu</span>" ג€” "Shabbos is the Name of the Holy One."</p>

<p>Therefore on Yom Kippur the vav is completed in ultimate perfection ג€” more than all other Yamim Tovim of the year ג†’ most closely attached to the N'kudah. When the vav (heart/Luchos) is completed in perfection, it easily receives the light of the N'kudah (yud/Aseres HaDibros/kedushah of Shabbos). Therefore on <span class="key-term">Yom Kippur the second Luchos were given</span> ג€” the tikkun of the original shivray Luchos. The essential aspect of Yom Kippur is to rectify shivray Luchos (from which come all sins): the vav illuminates in completeness (Luchos in completeness ג€” the opposite of shivray Luchos) ג†’ receiving the light of the N'kudah (Aseres HaDibros) etched upon them, enduring forever, the letters never flying off again ג†’ all avonos are forgiven: all cherpas laiv are nullified through the light of the N'kudah = the tikkun of everything.</p>

<h3>ֲ§31</h3>

<p>Therefore we read Torah on <span class="key-term">Monday and Thursday</span> ג€” these are the days on which Moshe Rabbainu ascended and descended to receive the <span class="transliteration">Luchos Sh'niyos</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the second Luchos]</span>, given on Yom HaKippurim. The second Luchos = tikkun sh'viras kailim. Therefore we read Torah on these days ג€” the essential Kri'as HaTorah is for this tikkun of shivray Luchos.</p>

<h3>ֲ§32</h3>

<p>Therefore the custom on <span class="key-term">Simchas Torah</span> (Sh'mini Atzeres) is to call up all of Yisrael to the Torah. Then we complete the Torah ג€” the comprehensive N'kudah comprised of all the N'kudos in every person of Yisrael. The N'kudah of each person of Yisrael corresponds to the <span class="transliteration">os</span> <span class="bracket-def">[letter]</span> he has in the Torah (where his neshamah is rooted). Therefore each and every one is called up ג€” so the N'kudah illuminates in him from the Torah being completed then.</p>

<p>This takes place on Sh'mini Atzeres (Simchas Torah) ג€” the <span class="bold-concept">conclusion and end of all the Yamim Tovim</span>. All the tikkun of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim is completed in Sh'mini Atzeres. Therefore it is called <span class="transliteration">Atzeres</span> ג€” a term of <span class="transliteration">asaifah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[gathering]</span>: it gathers and assembles all the tikkunim made throughout the year (especially the Yamim Tovim, Rosh HaShanah, and Yom HaKippurim) ג€” all is gathered in Sh'mini Atzeres where all tikkunim are completed. It is the finale of Sukkos ג€” the last Regel called <span class="transliteration">Chag Ha'Asif T'kufas HaShanah</span> ג€” "the festival of the ingathering, the circuit of the year" ג€” encompassing the entire year.</p>

<p>Therefore we complete the Torah then and call all of Yisrael ג€” the essential tikkun is through the N'kudah (= the entirety of Torah completed then). Each person of Yisrael receives his N'kudah from Torah ג†’ it illuminates in all of Yisrael, each in his fellow ג†’ the tikkun of the entire year is completed. Therefore the <span class="key-term">simchah is exceedingly great</span>: all the N'kudos of all of Yisrael illuminate then, each in the other ג†’ the light increases more and more through the collective illumination of N'kudos one into another ג†’ the simchah grows immensely. All the N'kudos are in the aspect of Tzaddik ג€” and by the Tzaddik is the essential simchah: "<span class="transliteration">Or Tzaddikim yismach</span>" ג€” "The light of the Tzaddikim rejoices" <span class="source">(Mishlei 13)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Simchu Tzaddikim baHashem</span>" ג€” "Rejoice, Tzaddikim, in Hashem" <span class="source">(Tehillim 97)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">V'Tzaddikim yism'chu ya'altzu</span>" ג€” "And the Tzaddikim shall rejoice and exult" <span class="source">(Tehillim 68)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Or zarua laTzaddik ul'yishray laiv simchah</span>" ג€” "Light is sown for the Tzaddik, and for the upright of heart ג€” simchah" ג€” <span class="key-term">Amain v'Amain</span>.</p>

<h3>ֲ§33</h3>

<p>This is what the Sages said <span class="source">(Megillah, ch. "HaKorai Omaid")</span> regarding Kri'as HaTorah: "<span class="transliteration">Hanay sh'loshah, chamishah, shiv'ah ג€” k'neged Bircas Kohanim</span>" ג€” "These three, five, seven ג€” correspond to <span class="key-term">Bircas Kohanim</span>." The essential Kri'as HaTorah b'tzibbur is to draw and illuminate the holy N'kudah where the holy love dwells ג†’ through this, shivray Luchos are rectified. This is the aspect of Bircas Kohanim (as explained at length above). Therefore we say Bircas Kohanim after Bircas HaTorah every day ג€” for the essential tikkun is in the aspect of Bircas Kohanim: arousing the holy love dwelling near the holy N'kudah drawn through Kri'as HaTorah ג€” through which <span class="bold-concept">everything is rectified</span>.</p>

<p class="no-indent" style="text-align:center;font-size:13px;color:#999;margin-top:30px;">Hilchos N'si'as Kapayim, Halacha 6 is included in Hilchos Bircas HaShachar, Halacha 5.</p>

<div style="margin-top:40px;padding-top:20px;border-top:2px dashed #c8a96e;">
